# thread-split.allow — the files that may name engine memory, each with its class, its
# scope and the argument for it (tools/thread-split-check.sh; frame-packet-exchange §10).
#
# DEFAULT-DENY: only a file listed here may name an engine address, include the engine
# header, probe with IsBad*Ptr or add an offset to the main pointer, with comments
# stripped. The list only SHRINKS: a change that converts a file to the packet removes
# its line in the same diff. Adding a
# line loosens the rule for every future edit of that file and is the owner's decision,
# taken in a review — never a session's way past a red build.
#
# Classes, one per line after the path:
#   publisher         game-thread code inside an engine call, at a site that owns the
#                     memory it reads (observers, the packet's fill, detour installers
#                     that byte-match at attach)
#   session-reader    render thread, data that lives for the session and is never
#                     rewritten (GAF banks, the cursor table); NOT fonts
#   fenced            render thread, per-LEVEL assets (model templates, the tile set)
#                     under tagpu_reclaim's teardown fence; retired by the asset channel,
#                     the decision after landing 5
#   tooling           trigger-gated readers (peek, catalogues, the GUI snapshot, the
#                     weapon dump, scenario detection); out of the exchange's scope.
#                     THEY RUN ON THE GAME THREAD, from the engine's own flip
#                     (tagpu_gui_hook.c's before_flip) rather than from
#                     tagpu_overlay_draw -- which is what lets them reach the GDI backend
#                     -- so they read engine memory from the thread that owns it.
#   pure-engine-code  render thread executes an engine FUNCTION over our buffers, with
#                     the disassembly range that shows it reads only its arguments and
#                     writes only its destination
#   engine-map        the header the addresses live in (publisher files include it)
#   to-convert:N      render-thread reader of per-frame or per-map engine state, to be
#                     converted in landing N of the migration table
#
# Scope, one word after the class (tools/sim-state-check.sh; inc/tagpu_simreg.h):
#   sim               the simulation's own code: every writable static, heap table and TLS
#                     slot it has is declared to the demo recorder's registry, and its memory,
#                     TLS indices, page changes and levers come through the registry's
#                     wrappers. A source file only: a header's statics belong to the objects
#                     that include it.
#   presentation      everything else: renderers, the UI, the camera, the menu, reclamation,
#                     scenarios and catalogues, the TADR takeover. Out of the recorder's
#                     scope, so marking a file that holds simulation state `presentation` is
#                     a loosening, and the owner's decision.
#
# The argument after `#` is what the reviewer checks, not what the script checks.

inc/tagpu_engine.h       engine-map          presentation  # the addresses, for publisher files only
src/tagpu_packet_pub.c   publisher           presentation  # the frame packet's fill, from DrawGameScreen's `after` at 0x4969D2; the font copy at hook 8; the level-end packet; and LoadMap's join 0x483B53 on the LOADER thread (mapfeat_at_load): LoadMap's own frame, the FeatureDefs 0x421F20 has just counted and the grid's heights LoadMap has just written, read after those writes in LoadMap's own order on its own thread, before the level's first in-play draw; the list it builds is ours, and reaches the game thread only through the interlocked hand-over slot
src/tagpu_markown.c      publisher           presentation  # the marker block's call-site redirects on the game thread; hook 8 latches the font through the publisher
src/tagpu_menu.c         publisher           presentation  # the render-options screen: GUI calls on the game thread from the DrawGameScreen observer
src/tagpu_replays.c      publisher           presentation  # the shell's Replays screen and the main menu's REPLAYS button, on the game thread inside the engine's shell calls: GUI_Load 0x4AACAE writes the main menu's parsed records before its stage 1, the PCX loader's return 0x429290 the FrontendX surface it has just decoded before its first copy, the builder 0x42644C the screen's OnCommand slot it has just written, and the list draw 0x4A1B40 draws into our own screen's panel surface; the OnCommands are the engine calling us from its pump. It holds no simulation state
src/tagpu_hud.c          publisher           presentation  # HUD scale: an observer on the loading-screen flip 0x4288D0 gated on the return address 0x498242 writes the viewport rect's R/B/W/H on the game thread; the one word it publishes is its own
src/tagpu_reclaim.c      publisher           presentation  # FreeObjectState and the teardown wrap on the game thread; the fence itself
src/tagpu_gui_hook.c     publisher           presentation  # the UI observers and the flip publisher on the game thread; the probes are on its own arena
src/tagpu_gui_leaves.h   publisher           presentation  # observers on the game thread, all of them. The minimap picture main+0x1426B is alive for the whole level, not only inside BuildMinimapSurface 0x466780, so the packet's publisher decodes it on the level's first in-play draw. Nothing in this file runs on the loader thread
src/tagpu_order.c        publisher           presentation  # the order-marker snapshot at 0x469BFC, game thread: it resolves every unit pointer to an array SLOT and every def and weapon field to a number in the record, so the present half reads the packet's units, GUI colours and palette and dereferences nothing. The GAF sequences it carries are session assets tagpu_gaf.c resolves
src/tagpu_owndraw.c      publisher           presentation  # the composite detours' classifier on the game thread (0x459830/0x459C70)
src/tagpu_fxown.c        publisher           presentation  # effects call-site redirects and leaf detours on the game thread
src/tagpu_featown.c      publisher           presentation  # the feature leaf detour 0x46A610 on the game thread
src/tagpu_terrown.c      publisher           presentation  # the terrain and fog-overlay detours; the fog grid's lazy rebuild on the game thread
src/tagpu_fogwide.c      publisher           presentation  # the wide fog grid: built on the GAME thread inside an in-play draw — at the engine's own fog site when it is ours, else, on the Vulkan renderer, in the publisher's `after` of the same draw — after the level load that allocates the LOS and MAPPED maps and before the teardown (which frees MAPPED; the LOS counters are freed by the next load). It hands over nothing: the packet's publisher copies the grid out of it on this thread, in the same draw, so it needs no hand-over buffer, critical section or retire ring
src/tagpu_patches.c      publisher           sim           # byte patches applied at attach, before the engine runs; and the terrain window bound, which reads the pass's frame, the tile map, the tile set and the OFFSCREEN on every in-play draw, on the game thread, inside 0x483FA0 through its 0x484057 stub; and, not the game thread, the saved-feature restore on the LOADER thread: it reads and writes the feature grid's def words and refreshes the pathing maps around 0x424C00's one call at 0x43265A, and reads the grid and the FeatureDefs where 0x424C00 writes a saved record's state (0x4250C0, 0x425185) -- inside the level load, in the engine's own order on its own thread, before any unit is restored and before the level's first in-play draw; and at a seek on a level that plays a recording (tagpu_replay.c), on the game thread in the frame visit between two sim steps: tagpu_effects_clear destroys every particle object of the 10 layers at *(main+0x38D77) through its own destructor and empties the layer vectors, as the teardown 0x471DE0's first loop does, then zeroes the explosion count and re-initialises the debris records and the flying-piece slots (the raised build's own pools, or the stock build's at main+0x1491B, main+0x1AB9F with their blocks, and 0x511DF0), and, at that seek and at every capture point and frame visit of such a level, tagpu_patches_restored bounds the air pool under its lock (held from tagpu_patches_restore_begin over the replay's table write, C code alone), the hit delay queue and the ghost sweep's count; the render thread reads none of it
src/tagpu_suppress.c     publisher           presentation  # the DrawUnit detour's classifier on the game thread
src/tagpu_tracer.c       publisher           presentation  # the draw tracer's detours on the game thread
src/tagpu_cobtrace.c     publisher           sim           # the COB oracle's five sites inside the script engine, game thread
src/tagpu_datakeys.c     publisher           sim           # TADR section C's unit keys. Observers at the unit-data load's start 0x42D2E0, the FBI loader's entry 0x42BF40 and its read site 0x42BF97, and the COB checksum 0x4B6BA0, on the LOADER thread (the level's unit-data load) and on the GAME thread (the console's one-type reload 0x42D1F0): the section the loader holds, through the engine's own reader 0x4C48C0, and each script block's address and length; the records they write are ours. The ghost masks, on the GAME thread inside the in-play packet fill: the def array, MODEL_PTRS, each type's COB (def+0x18E) and template tree, all of the LEVEL, loaded before its first in-play draw and freed by its teardown on this thread, the COB also by a Reload on this thread; every read of a COB is bounded by its recorded length. The weapon keys (C2): their store written on the LOADER thread (emptied at 0x42E310, filled at 0x42E468) with a weapon's +0x111 noradar bit at 0x49E010, and read on the GAME thread by the targeting sites, which clear a slot's target through the engine's ClearTarget 0x48A0F0; nomapweaponalert's hit frame, blink hold and the save record's byte +0xB3 on the GAME thread (the sim tick, the dispatcher's 0x0B case, the unit saver), the hold restored on the LOADER thread inside the same load that emptied it. The render thread reads none of it: the masks reach it as a packet table
src/tagpu_weapons.c      publisher           sim           # the extra-weapons module: sim-side sites on the game thread; its def records written on the LOADER thread inside the unit-data load (emptied at 0x42D2E0's entry, filled at 0x42CEF2), before the level's first in-play tick, and at a console Reload on the game thread (the piece caches forgotten at 0x42BF40's entry, the whole record rewritten at 0x42CEF2 when the FBI opens); the WEAPON_FIRED receiver can also run during a load (the dispatcher 0x453D40 at 0x49852E and 0x49727D), reads those records bounded and can fill one piece cache, in an array never freed; at each capture point, frame visit and seek of a level that plays a recording, on the game thread between two sim steps, tagpu_weapons_restored bounds each def record def_rec can return (its count against its weapons and masks, its piece caches) after the rebuild has written them; the weapon dump is tooling
src/tagpu_recorder.c     publisher           presentation  # the demo recorder's capture, inside B2's stub at the sim step's unit-tick call 0x4954ED, on the GAME thread between two iterations: the unit array (bounded by the engine's own slot count, checked against its last-slot pointer), a live unit's mover (0x2F bytes the creates allocate and only the destructor frees, nulling +0x00 in the same call) and its two order lists through each node's next (every node made, linked and freed on this thread; a vtable test and a 4096-node cap end the recording rather than read on; a node's owned object read once for its vtable), the ten player records and their PlayerInfo blocks (nothing frees main's), and the declared SIM_STATE rows, each bounded by its declaration; for the header and the harness's dump also the unit-def and weapon names (UNITINFOCount of them, the weapon array's slots), the player names and alliance rows, the game options, the skirmish settings' start mode and map name, and GameingState's TNT path; and the features (the slice note's S3a): the feature grid, the wreck pool, their three list heads and the reproduction cursor, and the feature defs below their count, each checked at every capture against the ones the recording began with (the level's own, which only the teardown frees), a cell an argument names bounded by the grid's columns and rows and a record by the pool's count before anything is read there; and the globals and the projectiles (the slice note's S3b): the fields of the main block and .data replay-globals.tsv lists, each inside the main block's 0x3924D bytes or .data by the generator's check, and the projectile pool through main's pool pointer, checked at every capture against the one the recording began with, its count bounded by the pool's TAGPU_LIM_PROJ records (a count past them ends the recording) and its records read below the written extent, which is bounded by the same; and the explored map through main+0x14273, checked at every capture against the one the recording began with and read within the bytes LoadMap computes from the grid's columns and rows (a block only the level's end frees); for the harness's dump also the explored map, each player record's sight counters within the block the player init allocated at the size it stored beside it, and the main block and .data whole, both mapped for the process's life. An id row's pointer is never dereferenced but an order node's owned object: its ID is one more than its row in its table. The event hooks run on the GAME thread inside the engine's own calls (the create's exit, the destructor's entry, the three script broadcasts, the build-done, the state setter, the two replies, the nano query, emitters and handler calls, thirteen owner-only COB starts, the fire functions' 0x0D and area damage's 0x0E through tagpu_patches.c, the seven feature functions' entries and returns, the three footprint writers' entries and, on a level that plays a recording, their returns, and the feature code's six calls of the simulation RNG and the destructor's explosion call 0x486D50, redirected to stubs that call the engine's own function): they read the call's own arguments and registers no further than the engine does, name a unit only once its pointer is a slot by arithmetic, and a call on any other thread is counted and not recorded; the five fire functions' network tests are released while recording, which builds the packet on their own stack and runs the extra weapons' slot-index splice, which writes no simulation state (tagpu_weapons.c, cb_slot_index). The recording ends at the teardown wrap's entry, before the cascade frees the array, and does not start without that wrap. It hands images to the writer thread through a ring; the writer, tagpu_recwriter.c, reads no engine memory and is not listed
src/tagpu_puppet.c       publisher           sim           # the puppet host's table (tagpu_puppet.h): whether this level plays a recording, which records are puppet seats and getter 75's answer for each, SIM_CONTENT (decision D3), written by the seat fill on the GAME thread in the SKIRMISH Start before the loader thread exists and cleared by the teardown wrap on the game thread; read by COB's getter on the game thread and the loader thread, and by the loader's start-unit stub, between those two points. And its gates: prologue gates landed at attach on twenty engine functions (the damage apply 0x489CE0, Send_UnitDeath 0x4864B0, COB's SET 0x480B20 at its second instruction 0x480B26, ATTACH 0x481340 and DROP 0x4813B0, the replies 0x47F780 and 0x47F850, the meteor shower 0x437DE0, typed commands 0x417B50, the debug keys 0x4956C0, the resource transfer 0x464C60, the save 0x4326B0, and the feature tick 0x424050, the spawn 0x423C50, the destroy 0x4246B0, FeatureDie 0x423550, the burn 0x4233A0, the damage 0x4244B0 and the swap 0x423710, and the skirmish elimination 0x47BD70, refused only while a seek rebuilds the level). A predicate reads nothing but the mode unless the level plays a recording; then, on the thread the engine calls the function on, it reads the call's own arguments and registers, the unit array's bounds from main (the level holds the array until its teardown, whose wrap ends the mode first), a unit's owner byte only once its pointer is a slot by arithmetic, and a COB object's unit as the function itself reads it. And the footprint writers' undo, from the recorder's observers at the stamp 0x47CC30, the clear 0x47D0E0 and the re-claim 0x47C790 on the GAME thread: on a level that plays a recording it saves the unit's footprint cells, the unit a slot by arithmetic first and the rectangle clipped to the grid's W and H from main, and puts their unit slots and yard bit back at the call's return
src/tagpu_replay.c       publisher           presentation  # playing a recording back, on the GAME thread: the SKIRMISH Start's calls of the seat fill (0x47B092) and of the settings persist (0x47B09C), in the Start handler, read and write the ten player records and their PlayerInfo blocks (main's, which nothing frees), the skirmish settings the Start has just filled from, GameingState's mode and the objective the .ota read at 0x47AF77 armed; the loader's two start-unit calls (0x497963, 0x497ACD) read only the puppet flag, written before the loader thread exists; the step inside B2's stub at the unit-tick call 0x4954ED, between two iterations, reads the level's counts, GameingState's TNT path and GameTime, and writes V's sight counters and its bit in the explored map at the first step, then asks for (main+0x142F1 bit 2) and runs the minimap's fog composite 0x466C20 on the images the level init makes (0x4919C3) and only the teardown frees (0x491BAE), V's energy and metal (0 at the first step), each unit's control group only through the engine's setter 0x480250, the group and the unit's current one each bounded by the owner's 10-entry table, the feature grid's cells and the tick's input written back from the next iteration's world, the wreck pool's records, the reproduction cursor within the level's grid, pool and counts, and calls the feature functions with recorded arguments bounded by the grid, the feature defs and the weapons (THE FEATURES); the recorder's stub at the destructor's explosion call 0x486D50 makes those calls for the death being replayed, inside the destructor; the observer on the settings save 0x430F00 exchanges the settings with the player's around each save on the save's thread, the game thread (no call of it is on the loader thread), and only from the level's first step, when the loader has read them; the teardown wrap's entry puts the settings back, and its post hook, after 0x491B60 returns on the same thread, writes V's PlayerInfo back; the first call of B2's stub, at the capture point, and the frame visit before the in-play draw call 0x4969CD (paused frames included), both between two iterations, write every declared table that pairs with this build's declaration (as the seek does, below), the main block's and .data's fields replay-globals.tsv lists as `write` (each inside the main block or .data by the generator's check), LosType bits 0-2 with the engine's own reaction (every temporary sight record's expiry zeroed, the engine's count of them bounded by the 20 its add allows, then the expiry 0x482130 and the sight rebuild 0x4816A0, then V's sight again with the same composite), each live unit's water state (bounded by its enum) and sight stamp's fields through the removal 0x482090 and the stamp 0x482270 (a circle radius index bounded by the frame table's count) where they differ from the recording's, and the projectile pool through main's pool pointer, its count and extent bounded by the pool's TAGPU_LIM_PROJ records and every pointer formed from an ID within its table (the weapon array, the unit array, the pool), and the explored map within the bytes LoadMap computes from the host's grid, equal to the recording's, with LosType bit 3 cleared when an entry changed, and the frame visit reads and deletes the trigger file and runs a seek: after bounding the recording's world at the target (the feature lists, records and cells, the sort buckets and their chains, the explored map's size, the projectile pool, LosType's slot and the engine's count of temporary sight records), it empties the projectile pool, kills every unit through the destructor (C4's wrap, severity and corpse 0) with the skirmish elimination gated, drops the temporary sight records through 0x482130, resets the sight through 0x4816A0, sets GameTime, makes every recorded unit through the create, runs the step's appliers, sets each unit's water state (bounded by its enum), its state byte through the setter 0x48B090, and its sight stamp's fields through the removal 0x482090 and the stamp 0x482270 (a circle radius index bounded by the frame table's count), moves the units of the recorded buckets through the unlink 0x47CB00 and the move 0x47CB60, writes the wreck pool, its heads, the cursor and every cell (3DO models freed through 0x45AAA0 and made through 0x45A8D0, a cell's unit slots only when 0 or naming a live unit), clears the effects (tagpu_patches.c), writes every declared table that pairs with this build's declaration by name, stride, class, key, flags and layout hash within the rows the host holds now, an address only from an ID within its table (the air pool's lock held over the writes); in a round trip (`roundtrip <name> at <T>`, at the frame visit, on a puppet only) it first puts every static SIM_TRANSIENT declaration of the sim files back to its bytes saved at the playing's first frame visit (on the game thread, writing only bytes that differ; the ghost queue s_ghostQ/s_ghostN without s_ghostLock, since in play only the game thread pumps, tagpu_patches.c), and inside that table write fills every byte of every recorded declaration outside its layout's keep with 0xA5 and puts back from its copy every byte the write left at 0xA5, all before the lock is left; then the globals, the pool and the explored map as at a capture point, V's sight with the minimap's fog composite 0x466C20, runs the viewed record's radar pass 0x467440 once, as its 30-tick pass does, the seats' and V's 30-tick deadlines and every record's DisplayTimer, the notices' and the chat lines' resets 0x47EE30 and 0x463C80, and the pacing stamps. The recording is read by tagpu_recreader.c, which reads no engine memory and is not listed
src/tagpu_scenario.c     tooling             presentation  # both halves on the game thread: the applier on the tick detour, detection on the flip
src/tagpu_cat.c          tooling             presentation  # unit/feature catalogues behind tagpu_units.trigger / tagpu_features.trigger
src/tagpu_gui_snap.c     tooling             presentation  # the gadget-tree snapshot behind tagpu_ui.trigger
src/tagpu_zoom.c         publisher           presentation  # the camera clamp 0x41C3C0, the three target-clamp stubs 0x41C808/0x41C93B (the centre-ons' smooth arms) and 0x41CAF7 (the follow, in the stepper), the GetTPosition guard 0x498EF9 inside the mouse->world 0x498DA0, the debug-overlay call 0x468DBA inside DrawGameScreen, the minimap-rect and ScrollSpeed-save redirects, the command apply at the top of every in-play draw, and the scenario camera's hand-over from the flip observer (written by the next apply, or on the spot where no apply runs): every engine word here is read and written on the game thread; the render half (the levers, the anchor arithmetic, the predicted eye) reads only the packet
src/tagpu_vpwide.c       publisher           presentation  # the 0x498DA0 mouse->world redirect and the clip guard inside DrawGameScreen; the rect is widened and restored from the command apply, game thread; the true rect is read on the game thread only
src/tagpu_native.c       fenced              presentation  # the unit array, the poses, the option bits and the ground heights all come from the packet; what is left is per-LEVEL assets under tagpu_reclaim's teardown fence — the model templates behind MODEL_PTRS (whose base and bound the packet carries). Both fog grids come out of the packet. tagpu_native_owns_unit/owns_obj read the unit record and its def on the GAME thread, for the publisher and the owndraw classifier
src/tagpu_scaffold.c     fenced              presentation  # the units, the feature anchors, the map and sweep dimensions all come from the packet; the FeatureDef records and the GAF frames it stamps from are per-MAP assets under the fence
src/tagpu_render3do.c    fenced              presentation  # there is no write-back, and the shade table and build-state formulas take the packet's copies; the face material helpers still read the model template's face records and their GAF frames, and the atlas decodes the GAF frame of an effects model's face at the address the packet carries when its texels are not painted yet (tagpu_r3d_atlas_texels), per level, under the fence
src/tagpu_feat.c         fenced              presentation  # the anchors, their six height bytes, the map dimensions and the player and option bytes come from the packet; the FeatureDef records and the wreck records it draws GAF frames out of are per-MAP assets under the fence
src/tagpu_text.c         pure-engine-code    presentation  # the glyph blitter 0x4CCF60..0x4CD00E over ONE-GLYPH FONT OBJECTS OF OURS: it reads its arguments and writes its destination. Both text paths hand it a copy — the marker font's glyphs come in the frame packet and the UI's come as bits in the string op — so this file dereferences no engine font and carries no probe
src/tagpu_terr.c         fenced              presentation  # the tile set, the tile map and the height byte of every cell, per map under the fence; the level packet decides its fate
src/tagpu_posebake.c     fenced              presentation  # the per-type geometry bake walks the model template tree on the render thread
src/tagpu_gaf.c          session-reader      presentation  # GAF banks and the cursor table: loaded once, never rewritten. The "fx" bank the effects and particle passes draw from is one of them [VERIFIED 2026-09-12]: 0x429870 loads it and resolves every named sequence into main+0x147AB.., and its only caller is 0x49134D inside 0x491200, whose only caller is WinMain's 0x49EA62 — so those sequences are a SESSION asset, not a per-level one. This file also reads the frame HEADER for its callers (tagpu_gaf_frame_geom / _subframe), so a pass that only places a sprite dereferences nothing of its own
